=== layout: section

# DNA Fingerprinting\: Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms

=== layout: one-panel

# SNP Genotyping

@body

- SNP genotyping is the measurement of genetic variations of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs).
  - It is a form of genotyping, which is the measurement of more general genetic variation.
- A single-nucleotide polymorphism (pronounced “snips” ✂︎✂✂)
  - is a substitution of a single nucleotide at a specific position in the genome, that is present in a sufficiently large fraction of the population (e.g. 1% or more)

=== layout: two-panels

# Size of Different Genotypes

@left

- Diploid genome: about 6 billion base pairs.
- Exome: protein-coding exons; about 30 million base pairs.
- About 4-5 million SNPs per person relative to the reference genome.

@right

| Assay | Sites |
| --- | --- |
| Genome | 3,200,000,000 |
| All SNPs | 149,735,377 |
| Exome | 39,000,000 |
| Omni SNP | 2,381,000 |
| FTDNA | 720,350 |
| Ancestry | 664,000 |
| 23andMe | 654,027 |
| SNPedia | 111,300 |

=== layout: one-panel

